//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
//
// X-macro table of the mnemonics `dxbc-as` understands: the whole SM4/SM5
// instruction set, so that any DXBC shader body a test needs can be written
// as assembly text rather than as a hex DWORD listing (see the "dxbc-as"
// section of feme/docs/Design.md).
//
// Each entry is:
//   DXBC_OPCODE(EnumName, Mnemonic, Value, NumDst, NumSrc, Controls, Kind,
//               Flags)
//
// `Value` is the literal value of the corresponding
// D3D10_SB_OPCODE_TYPE/D3D11_SB_OPCODE_TYPE enumerator from Microsoft's
// public `d3d11TokenizedProgramFormat.hpp`, so the binary this tool emits
// uses the real, documented token values rather than ones invented for this
// tool.
//
// `Controls` holds the opcode-specific control bits ([23:11], plus the
// test-boolean bit 18) that the mnemonic spelling itself implies: several
// real opcodes are spelled as a family of mnemonics that differ only in
// those bits (`callc_z`/`callc_nz`, `resinfo`/`resinfo_rcp`/`resinfo_uint`,
// `dcl_resource_texture2d`/`dcl_resource_texture3d`/...), which keeps the
// grammar free of positional "mode" keywords.
//
// `NumDst`/`NumSrc` are how many destination and source operands the
// mnemonic takes; the split matters because a one-letter component suffix
// means a write mask on a destination but a single-component select on a
// source.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

#ifndef DXBC_OPCODE
#define DXBC_OPCODE(EnumName, Mnemonic, Value, NumDst, NumSrc, Controls,      \
                    Kind, Flags)
#endif

DXBC_OPCODE(Add, "add", 0, 1, 2, 0, Generic, OF_Saturable)
